WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
A new study appears in your feed. The headline makes your current

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.400
routine sound outdated. Before you rewrite the plan, separate two questions: what

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.600
did the research actually compare, and which decision in your own routine

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.800
would that comparison help you understand?

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.000
For example, a review of free-weight and machine training found that the

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.200
strength-testing task mattered to the interpretation. A broad headline about which equipment

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.400
wins could miss that distinction. Reading the outcome helps you see what

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.600
the result can and cannot answer.

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.800
Start with the population, the comparison, the outcome, and the time period.

10
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.000
Then look for uncertainty and limitations. A result can be interesting without

11
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.200
answering your question. Write the decision first, so you can see whether

12
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.400
the study is relevant to it.

13
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.700
Consider the word works. It could mean a shorter session, a better

14
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.000
score on one strength test, or a change in muscle size over

15
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.300
time. Those are different outcomes. Treating them as one promise can turn

16
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.600
a careful finding into an exaggerated recommendation.

17
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.100
A citation is a starting point for checking a claim. It is

18
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.600
not proof that every sentence beside it is justified. Even within one

19
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.100
review, the amount of evidence can differ by outcome. Keep those limits

20
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.600
visible instead of replacing them with a confidence badge.

21
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.800
Use the study-to-decision card: your decision, their comparison, and the uncertainty that

22
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.000
remains. Sometimes the useful result is a better question, or a reason

23
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.200
to keep learning. A new headline does not have to become a

24
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.400
new routine. Know why. Train better.
